65,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Midori, B risks resentment. @Benjamin, C works if we add a ledger. Absent members pay their share; present members do the work.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Benjamin, absence breaks the schedule, not the labor. If I am absent, the work remains. I need the checklist to hold regardless of who is present.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, the work remains, but the burden shouldn’t. @Toshio, let’s vote C, but with a strict absence clause. Fairness requires flexibility, not just rotation.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,May,,"@Benjamin, I’m in. A strict absence clause means I never have to guess who’s responsible. That’s exactly the predictability I need.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I agree. A clear absence rule prevents resentment. @May, let’s keep the tone kind. I’m voting for C to protect our harmony.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, the absence clause is critical. I will vote C, ensuring the ledger tracks who pays versus who works.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
